<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $f: C \rightarrow C'$ be an equivalence, so that the restriction maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC _{/C} \leftarrow \calC _{/f} \rightarrow \calC _{/C'} \]
</div>
    <p>
       are trivial fibrations of simplicial sets. Let $\calC ^{0}_{/f} = \calC ^{0} \times _{\calC } \calC _{/f}$, so that we have trivial fibrations 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC ^{0}_{/C} \stackrel{g}{\leftarrow } \calC ^{0}_{/f} \stackrel{g'}{\rightarrow } \calC ^{0}_{/C'}. \]
</div>
    <p>
       Consider the associated diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &  (\calC ^{0}_{/C})^{\triangleright } \ar [dr]^{F_{C}} &  \\ (\calC ^{0}_{/f})^{\triangleright } \ar [ur]^{G} \ar [dr]^{G'} &  &  \calD \\ &  (\calC ^{0}_{/C'})^{\triangleright } \ar [ur]^{F_{C'}} &  .} \]
</div>
    <p>
       This diagram does not commute, but the functors $F_{C} \circ G$ and $F_{C'} \circ G'$ are equivalent in the $\infty $-category $\calD ^{ (\calC ^{0}_{/f})^{\triangleright }}$. Consequently, $F_{C} \circ G$ is a $p$-colimit diagram if and only if $F_{C'} \circ G'$ is a $p$-colimit diagram (Proposition <a href="/tag/6AE167" data-tag="6AE167">4.3.1.5</a>). Since $g$ and $g'$ are cofinal, we conclude that $F_{C}$ is a $p$-colimit diagram if and only if $F_{C'}$ is a $p$-colimit diagram (Proposition <a href="/tag/432962" data-tag="432962">4.3.1.7</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>